{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassTheory.UniversalBraidBranching","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassTheory.UniversalBraidBranching","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Trace"],"declarations":15,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.convergence_bound_envelope_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.convergence_bound_envelope_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":374,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L374-L409","doc":"The BW convergence-bound **envelope** `C₀ · [2]_q^{-2(k-3)}` is\nnon-negative for `q > 1`, `C₀ > 0`.\n\n**This is not the convergence bound.** It says nothing about MAE(k)\n— no error quantity appears in the statement. It establishes only\nthat the expression proposed as an upper bound is itself\nnon-negative, which follows from `positivity`.\n\nThe claim the manuscript makes, `MAE(k) ≤ C₀ · [2]_q^{-2(k-3)}` with\n`C₀ ≈ 0.035%` the k=3 mass MAE, is **not formalised here and does not\nfollow from this**: MAE(k) is a numerical quantity of the mass table\nwith no definition in scope, so there is nothing for the inequality\nto constrain. Stating it would need MAE defined first.\n\nRenamed 2026-08-23 (bean `qou-lo1t`). It was `convergence_bound`,\nunder a docstring asserting the MAE inequality and three inline\ncomments elaborating a bound the statement never makes. The name and\nthe docstring were the whole of the claim.\n\nFor a convergence bound that *is* stated over the quantities it\nbounds, see `QOU.Archimedean.SkewSYT.ConvergenceBound` — the\nKerov–Vershik `|f_k - f_inf| ≤ C_q / (k-3)`, carried as a class with\na proved reflexivity instance. It is a different bound (`1/(k-3)`,\nnot exponential in `k`), so it is not this one restated. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.chebyshev_sl2_cube","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.chebyshev_sl2_cube","kind":"theorem","line":349,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L349-L372","doc":"The Chebyshev identity for traces of SL(2) powers:\ntr(M³) = t³ - 3t where t = tr(M).\nUsed to derive the Borromean character from the figure-eight.\n\nThe hypothesis `hCH` is the 2×2 Cayley–Hamilton relation\n`M² = t·M - det(M)·I` specialised to `det M = 1` (M ∈ SL(2)).\nFrom it: M³ = t·M² - M = (t²)·M - t·I - M, and taking traces\n(tr M = t, tr I = 2) gives tr(M³) = t³ - 3t. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.universal_braid_branching","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.universal_braid_branching","kind":"theorem","line":279,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L279-L347","doc":"The universal branching formula.\n\nFor any braid K on 3 strands, the character in irrep λ of H_k(q) is\ndetermined by the three base characters and the skew SYT counts.\n\nProof by induction on k:\nBase (k=3): ρ_μ↓H₃ = ρ_μ, so skewSYTCount μ μ = 1 (empty skew shape).\nStep (k→k+1): by Pieri rule + linearity of the trace. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:universal-braid-branching","label":"prop:universal-braid-branching","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.cast_pieri","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.cast_pieri","kind":"theorem","line":272,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L272-L277","doc":"Real-valued Pieri rule: `(f^{λ/μ} : ℝ)` is the sum of the real casts of\nthe removal counts. Cast of `pieri_skew_syt`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.sum_lin","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.sum_lin","kind":"theorem","line":263,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L263-L270","doc":"Linearity of the list sum over the three-term branching combination. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.base_enum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.base_enum","kind":"theorem","line":227,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L227-L261","doc":"The only weakly-decreasing lists of positive parts summing to `3` are the\nthree partitions of `3`. This is the base-case enumeration for the\nbranching from `H₃`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.oneCellRemovals_pres","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.oneCellRemovals_pres","kind":"theorem","line":137,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L137-L225","doc":"One-cell removal preserves the partition invariants: if `lam` is\nweakly decreasing with positive parts, then so is every `x ∈\noneCellRemovals lam`. Needed to apply the branching induction hypothesis\nto each removal. The corner condition `ri > lam[i+1]` is exactly what\nkeeps the decremented row above its successor after removal. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.skewSYTCount_eq_zero_of_ne_of_sum_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.skewSYTCount_eq_zero_of_ne_of_sum_le","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L125-L135","doc":"`f^{λ/μ} = 0` whenever `λ ≠ μ` and `λ.sum ≤ μ.sum` (in particular for\ndistinct equal-size shapes): one Pieri step drops every removal strictly\nbelow `mu.sum`, then `skewSYTCount_eq_zero_of_sum_lt` applies. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.skewSYTCount_eq_zero_of_sum_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.skewSYTCount_eq_zero_of_sum_lt","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L109-L123","doc":"If the outer shape is strictly smaller than the inner, there is no skew\nSYT: `f^{λ/μ} = 0` when `λ.sum < μ.sum`. Proof by strong induction on\n`lam.sum` via the Pieri recursion (every removal stays below `mu.sum`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.pieri_skew_syt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.pieri_skew_syt","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L94-L107","doc":"Pieri rule for skew SYT counts: adding one cell decomposes.\nf^{λ⁺/μ} = Σ_{λ: λ⁺ covers λ in Young's lattice} f^{λ/μ}\n\nThe non-trivial content is that the paths μ → λ⁺ in Young's lattice\ndecompose over the intermediate λ (paths μ → λ followed by one step\nλ → λ⁺) — the saturated-chain recursion of a graded poset. The\nhypothesis `lambda_plus ≠ mu` excludes the base case `f^{μ/μ} = 1`,\nwhere the right-hand sum is empty. Here it is the defining recursion of\n`skewSYTCount` (the else-branch), specialised away from the base case. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.skewSYTCount_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.skewSYTCount_self","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L90-L92","doc":"SYT count of an empty skew shape is 1: `f^{μ/μ} = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.skewSYTCount","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.skewSYTCount","kind":"def","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L71-L88","doc":"Number of standard Young tableaux of skew shape `lambda / mu`, with\npartitions presented as weakly-decreasing `List ℕ` row lengths.\n\nDefined by the constructive corner-removal recursion in Young's lattice:\n`f^{μ/μ} = 1` (base case, empty skew shape), and for `λ⁺ ≠ μ`,\n`f^{λ⁺/μ} = Σ_{λ: λ⁺ covers λ} f^{λ/μ}` — the sum over one-cell removals.\nTermination is by `lam.sum`, which strictly decreases at each removal\n(`oneCellRemovals_sum_lt`). This standalone `List`-based definition mirrors\nthe `Partition`-typed `QOU.Archimedean.SkewSYT.skewSYTCount` and lets this\ndocumentation-mirror sibling compile without depending on the library. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.oneCellRemovals_sum_lt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.oneCellRemovals_sum_lt","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L66-L69","doc":"Every one-cell removal is strictly smaller (in size). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.oneCellRemovals_sum_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.oneCellRemovals_sum_succ","kind":"theorem","line":39,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L39-L64","doc":"Removing one corner cell drops the size (sum of parts) by exactly one:\n`x.sum + 1 = lam.sum` for every `x ∈ oneCellRemovals lam`. This is the\ntermination measure for `skewSYTCount`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.oneCellRemovals","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.oneCellRemovals","kind":"def","line":27,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/universal-braid-branching.lean#L27-L37","doc":"One-cell removals of a partition (weakly-decreasing row list): the lists\nobtained by removing a single corner cell — row `i` loses a cell exactly\nwhen the result stays weakly decreasing (`row i > row (i+1)`). A row\nemptied by the removal is dropped. These are the partitions λ covered by\nλ⁺ in Young's lattice. ","signatureChunk":0}]}